# Bubble Sort in C

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, temp;
    int swapped;

    for (i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        swapped = 0;

        // Last i elements are already in place
        for (j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap adjacent elements
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }

        // If no swaps occurred, array is already sorted
        if (!swapped)
            break;
    }
}

void printArray(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        printf("%d ", arr[i]);
    printf("\n");
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {64, 34, 25, 12, 22, 11, 90};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);

    printf("Unsorted array: ");
    printArray(arr, n);

    bubbleSort(arr, n);

    printf("Sorted array:   ");
    printArray(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

## How It Works

| Step | Description |
|------|-------------|
| **Outer loop** | Runs `n-1` times (at most). Each pass places the next largest element in its correct position. |
| **Inner loop** | Compares adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. |
| **`swapped` flag** | Optimization: if no swaps occur in a pass, the array is already sorted and we exit early. |

## Example Output
```
Unsorted array: 64 34 25 12 22 11 90
Sorted array:   11 12 22 25 34 64 90
```

